  "text": "Recent investigations have renewed interest in how the chemical character of soil influences where plants grow. A simple field technique for measuring soil acidity and alkalinity has made it easier to link these chemical levels with plant patterns. Studies in various eastern locales showed that many plant species are confined to soils within narrow pH ranges. Because few species appear throughout all stages of ecological succession, it is reasonable to expect a clear connection between soil pH and the sequence of plant communities. As succession advances, the soil’s chemical balance should shift in step with the changing vegetation. To test this idea, 626 soil samples were collected from the different successional stages present in a central U.S. region and analyzed for their acidity/alkalinity levels."
